<<~ ahu slot=`#/b-privilege-custody-first`>>

!! B. Privilege, custody first

!!! B1. What page-realm code reaches today (the custody layer read)

Every row here belongs to the ''origin'', not to a thread. Code anywhere in the page realm (the main thread where the mirror runs) reaches it with `indexedDB.open` or `navigator.storage.getDirectory()`, whichever thread wrote it.

| Store | Contents at rest | Read gains | Write/delete gains |h
| IDB `lares:vessel` / `keystore`, before a slot binds | ''cleartext'' device Ed25519 seed (`vessel-key`, hex `signingKey`), veil Archives, place seed (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:9-10, 216-227`; `browser-custody.ts:4-8`) | ''total'': sign as the vessel, decrypt veiled docs | swap keys, brick the vessel |
| same, once a slot binds | `sealed/<row>` AEAD under the tree key; `custody-slots` holds salts, nonces and wrapped bytes (`browser-custody.ts:8-13`) | ciphertext only, so an offline attack on the passphrase | delete the tree (a refusal destroys nothing, but an attacker's delete does) |
| persona-root seeds | ''cleartext by ruling'' (SLIP-0010 needs bytes); the PRF wrap rides //beside// the cleartext, never instead (`seed-wrap-prf.ts:5-11`) | ''total'' for every face that root derives | same |
| the ''unlocked tree key'' | page memory, a module-level `Map` (`browser-custody.ts:96`) | not by reference, but same-realm code can ''poison `crypto.subtle`, `IDBObjectStore.prototype`, or `document` keydown before the unlock'' and catch the passphrase or the key | — |
| IDB `<idbName>:repo` | the Automerge repo, via `IndexedDBStorageAdapter` (`open-browser-vessel.ts:565`) and the per-wiki island stores (`browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:39-41`). Not sealed by this adapter (UNVERIFIED whether a sealing layer wraps it upstream) | every wiki's truth in the clear | ''forge or corrupt truth'' under the vessel's own actor |
| OPFS CAS dir | engine and plugin blobs by CID; re-verified by hash before reuse (`browser-genesis.ts:60-95`) | public bytes | denial only (a tampered blob refuses boot) |
| `localStorage` continuity marker | `founding` / `founded` (`browser-vessel-identity.ts:247-274`) | little | flip it and the boot reads `local-storage-lost` |
| `sessionStorage` crash records | the mirror's evidence | little | force skinny |

''Net:'' under "privilege stays open", a person's TW5 plugin ''already holds the vessel's signing key'' on an unslotted vessel, and every persona-root seed on every vessel. Sealing fixes the first only at rest. A live plugin can then wait for the unlock in the same realm.

So the question does not ask whether the mirror holds keys. ''The realm that runs the mirror holds the keys' storage.'' That remains true whatever "browser mode" fixes.

!!! B2. The options against every vessel setup

Setups: ''(1)'' a browser taking a port onto a node's own box; ''(2)'' an anon-device browser vessel; ''(3)'' a fleet of equivalent vessels; ''(4)'' a user-class cap-stack holding no node infrastructure.

| Option | What it buys | (1) port onto a node | (2) anon-device vessel | (3) fleet | (4) no node infra | Cost |h
| ''Accept full plugin trust'' (Obsidian: plugins hold the app's full access, and Obsidian "cannot technically limit" them, https://obsidian.md/help/plugin-security; VS Code: Workspace Trust "can't prevent a malicious extension…", https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editing/workspaces/workspace-trust) | nothing; it names the stance | tolerable: keys live on the node, and the page holds a port | ''every key in reach'' | each vessel exposed alike | worst: the browser IS the vessel | zero build; an honest label at plugin install |
| ''Key-side inversion'' (Privy: an isolated cross-origin iframe holds the key, rebuilt in memory only, signatures out, https://docs.privy.io/guide/security/; Turnkey's iframe stamper, https://docs.turnkey.com/sdks/advanced/iframe-stamper; Coinbase passkey popups, https://docs.base.org/identity/smart-wallet/features/single-sign-on) | custody leaves the app's origin; a plugin can ''ask'' for a signature but never ''read'' a key | moot: the node holds custody | ''fits'', but the custody origin's storage gets ''partitioned'' under the app's top-level site (see B3); Safari also partitions a same-site subdomain (WebKit 225297) and makes third-party storage ''ephemeral'' | each vessel's app origin and key origin pair alike | ''fits best'', but the partition trap hits hardest here | a second origin to serve (DNS, TLS: see the secure-context→DNS-01 memo); a signing RPC with per-request intent display (a plugin can still ask for a signature the person never meant) |
| ''Inversion, top-level variant'' (the key origin opened as a ''popup/top-level'' window, Coinbase-style) | dodges partitioning: top-level storage stays first-party | moot | ''fits''; one popup per unlock | same | fits | UX: a popup per signing session; popup blockers |
| ''App on a sandbox origin'' (the mirror's //page// moves to a user-content origin, the custody origin stays top-level; web.dev's pattern, https://web.dev/articles/securely-hosting-user-data; Observable, https://observablehq.com/@observablehq/security-model) | the mirror realm never shares an origin with custody | moot | fits | fits | fits | in effect the sandboxed iframe (lean B) that the operator ruled Mu on |
| ''SES / Hardened JS'' (`lockdown`, Compartments, https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/ses@2.2.0/README.md; LavaMoat in MetaMask, https://lavamoat.github.io/about/runtime-environment/) | per-plugin confinement if endowments exclude `window`/`indexedDB` | — | — | — | — | ''breaks TW5 as written'': modules expect bare `$tw`, `document`, and `window` through the DOM (`node.ownerDocument.defaultView` leaks `indexedDB`). TW5 mutates prototypes. A DOM membrane stands unbuilt anywhere this seat found. Figma tried a Realms shim on the main thread and ''left it after a shim escape'' for QuickJS-in-WASM (https://www.figma.com/blog/how-we-built-the-figma-plugin-system/). Refuse. |
| ''Trusted Types + CSP'' (Baseline Feb 2026: Firefox 148, Chromium 83, Safari 26, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Trusted_Types_API) | gates //injection// sinks (`innerHTML`, `eval`, `script.src`) | — | — | — | — | does ''not'' stop a plugin already running from `indexedDB.open`. TW5 ''evaluates plugin modules with `new Function`/`eval`'' (`boot.js` module executor), so a strict policy refuses plugins unless one named policy passes them; that turns the policy into a plugin-install gate, which has value as a ''choke point''. Worth it as hygiene (wikitext → HTML), not as custody. |
| ''Origin-per-wiki'' | each wiki's realm holds only its own wiki's storage | moot | each origin partitions under the app; per-wiki subdomains need a PSL entry to count as separate sites, and ''any'' custody on any of them still meets the trap | — | — | heavy DNS/TLS; breaks cross-wiki transclusion inside one page |

!!! B3. The storage-partition trap (current rules)

* ''Chrome'' (on since 115): the key = the frame's origin + the top-level site + an ''ancestor bit'' set when any frame in the chain is cross-site. IndexedDB, OPFS, localStorage, Cache, BroadcastChannel, SharedWorker, ''Web Locks'' and third-party service workers all partition (https://privacysandbox.google.com/cookies/storage-partitioning). The same origin opened top-level and opened framed under the app's site ''read different IndexedDBs''. A same-site chain stays unpartitioned. `requestStorageAccess({all:true})` (Chrome 125+) returns a //handle// to unpartitioned storage; it prompts unless the two sites form a Related Website Set (https://privacysandbox.google.com/blog/saa-non-cookie-storage).
* ''Firefox'' (State Partitioning / Total Cookie Protection): keyed on the top-level site; covers IDB, localStorage, Cache, workers and BroadcastChannel. A Storage Access grant holds per top-level site for 30 days (https://hacks.mozilla.org/introducing-state-partitioning/).
* ''Safari'':
** Third-party localStorage and IDB stand ''partitioned and ephemeral'' (https://webkit.org/tracking-prevention/).
** ''WebKit bug 225297 (open)'': a same-eTLD+1 subdomain iframe also gets its own partition (https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=225297).
** ''The 7-day cap'' deletes script-writable storage after seven days of use without interaction. Home Screen web apps stand exempt and hold storage separate from Safari's.
** Safari 17: an origin's quota is 60% of disk; LRU eviction; `persist()` granted by heuristic; ''cross-origin frames get 10% of the main frame's quota'' (https://webkit.org/blog/14403/updates-to-storage-policy/). Whether `persist()` beats the 7-day cap: UNVERIFIED.
** The house's own probe already reads the eviction class (`packages/lararium-browser/src/secure-context-gate.ts:154-176`).

''What the trap means for the house:''
* A key origin ''framed'' under the app keeps a keystore separate from the same key origin opened ''top-level'' (and from the Home Screen app's).
* On Safari the framed one also evaporates after seven idle days, which ''destroys keys unless a slot backs them up''. That runs straight into the RECOVERY ruling.
* Any framed-custody design therefore needs custody rows that the ''top-level'' context owns, or a re-derivation path (PRF, paper).

